{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 5,
  "simulation_id": "sim-04",
  "member_id": "olsson-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B031",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and experimental development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B018",
    "B019"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I change my preferred configuration from B018 to B031. Optical lattice clocks constitute a clearly identifiable atomic-physics invention: the magic-wavelength lattice made it possible to interrogate neutral-atom ensembles at exceptional precision without the limiting differential light shifts. The resulting capability is experimentally mature, independently reproduced, and has already opened direct measurements of gravitation and fundamental physics. Most importantly for final attribution, Katori's foundational concept and Ye's decisive experimental development provide a more secure and compact allocation of original credit than I can presently establish for qPlus microscopy.",
    "case_against": "The committee must not mistake exceptional metrology or a possible future redefinition of the second for the prize-worthy achievement. The citation must rest on the discrete physical breakthrough of the optical lattice clock and its demonstrated measurement regime. We should also confirm the historical boundary between the two indispensable contributions and the broader international programme of subsequent accuracy improvements.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that lattice clocks are already mature because independent laboratories have used them as precision probes of gravitation and fundamental physics, rather than merely as prospective standards technology.",
        "response": "I agree. This addresses my prior concern that the most consequential applications might remain ahead: the experimentally demonstrated ability to turn frequency comparisons into sensitive physical measurements is already a durable achievement. The citation should nevertheless avoid relying on future standards policy."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson emphasizes that Katori and Ye offer an unusually focused conceptual and experimental attribution, while warning that the prize should recognize a new quantum-control regime rather than a sustained improvement in timekeeping.",
        "response": "I find that distinction persuasive. It brings B031 into close alignment with the criterion of rewarding the original experimental advance that opens a new domain of observation. The magic-wavelength approach is the physical invention; the later clock-performance race is not what is being recognized."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson supports B026 for its connected theory-experiment arc and cautions that B031 may be viewed as precision engineering rather than a discrete discovery.",
        "response": "That remains the strongest reservation about B031. Yet the physical removal of the light-shift limitation for many trapped neutral atoms is more than incremental engineering, and its reproducible experimental consequences are unusually clear. B026 remains a major alternative, but its broader topological-insulator attribution boundary is less settled on the supplied record."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the historical record confirms that Katori and Ye alone encompass the indispensable original conceptual and experimental contributions to the cited lattice-clock breakthrough.",
      "Whether the final citation can precisely distinguish the foundational atomic-physics invention from later international improvements in clock accuracy and applications.",
      "Whether B018's qPlus priority and the roles of Meyer and Gross can be established sufficiently sharply for it to supersede B031."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No connection to the proposed laureates is apparent from the supplied profile. Göran Johansson remains a committee colleague at Chalmers Physics, but no candidate-related conflict is identified for B031."
  }
}
